# __NAME__

A thin OpenCapture app generated by `atlas new-app`. Brand, integration configuration,
per-install storage keys and bounded native picture adapters live here. OpenCine owns the
complete operator scene, connection/saved-camera model, command admission and pending edits,
settings, media catalog/cache, playback state and cover/item lifetime.

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `opencine-pins.txt`, `scripts/checkout-opencine.sh` | Exact shared commit and sibling checkout |
| `ios/project.yml` | Shipping, SIMULATED, unit-test and installed UI-test targets |
| `ios/__NAME__/`, `Android/app/src/main` | Brand and complete shared scene composition |
| `ios/__NAME__/Integrations.swift`, `Android/app/src/camera` | Shipping integration/native adapter factories |
| `ios/Simulated`, `Android/app/src/simulated` | Isolated public-contract FakeIntegration, synthetic source/media and SDK adapters |
| `ios/Tests`, `Android/app/src/testSimulated` | Route, controls, replacement/capability, persistence, media and retired-callback contracts |
| `ios/UITests`, `Android/app/src/androidTestSimulated` | Installed full flow and actual native-picture assertions |
| `scripts/check-simulated.sh` | Reproducible generated-app acceptance and evidence binding |
| `atlas-registration.example.json` | Optional unregistered example with explicit URL/ref/commit placeholders |
| `.github/workflows` | Acceptance CI and shipping-only manual release builds |

## Build and verify

Use macOS with Xcode, an installed iPhone 16 Pro simulator runtime and XcodeGen for iOS.
Android requires JDK 21, the Android SDK/NDK versions in the pinned OpenCine catalog,
platform tools and an explicitly selected API 29+ device or private emulator. The generated
source already contains the Gradle wrapper. `scripts/checkout-opencine.sh` fetches the exact
shared pin only when the required sibling checkout is absent; its existing-checkout rules
preserve local work. A fresh checkout requires the pinned commit to be available remotely.

```sh
scripts/checkout-opencine.sh
OUT="$PWD/build/SIMULATED-ios" scripts/check-simulated.sh ios
# Choose a target explicitly; the script never selects the default connected Android device.
ANDROID_SERIAL=your-selected-serial OUT="$PWD/build/SIMULATED-android" scripts/check-simulated.sh android
# Normal CI provisions and retires its own fresh emulator instead.
TEMPLATE_EMULATOR=1 OUT="$PWD/build/SIMULATED-ci" scripts/check-simulated.sh android
```

Each invocation needs a fresh `OUT`. The recipe builds shipping Debug/Release and the
SIMULATED developer variant, generates a second actual companion app with a separate bundle
and application ID, installs it, then runs eight contract and three installed UI scenarios.
It verifies discovery, saved connect, an actual moving decoded picture, ISO reports, confirmed
record start/stop, settings, media, native playback, cover/back navigation, disconnect, cached
offline playback and relaunch. Additional installed scenarios retire an open ISO editor when
its descriptor disappears and admit a picture-only camera without record/ISO controls.
Unit scenarios replace the connection during an edit/transfer and reject retired player callbacks.
Covers and rotation must retain the same live native owner; leaving and reopening a clip must
create a fresh playback owner. The companion app must begin with empty saved-camera storage.

The recipe keeps command logs, actual test reports, native-picture screenshots and pixel
measurements. `source-manifest.json` binds the shared commit, working-source hashes, both
generated apps and the actual built binaries. CI uploads these outputs even on failure.
Native frames and phone runtime checks use the packaged moving H.264 fixture, not a camera.
All scenarios and data are SIMULATED. Simulator/emulator success does not qualify physical
camera behavior, and an unsigned build is not an integrated or released app.

## Add a real integration

The shipping integration list starts empty. The complete shared scene remains usable for
pairing, saved-camera management and settings; no connected source is advertised until an
integration is configured. This is the documented production path:

1. Add the integration's existing OpenCine product/module to `ios/project.yml` and Android's
   composite substitutions/dependencies. Add the required OS usage strings and entitlements.
2. Construct its public integration with app storage/identity/transport values in the shipping
   `integrations()` factory. Return `AppIntegration(integration, slots: ...)` with only typed
   integration-specific pairing/features; capabilities and common controls remain shared.
3. Replace the shipping `operatorPicture` factory with the native source adapter for the
   connected session. Pass the shared assist effects/render plan and HDR preference into
   the existing shared native-runtime owner; borrow its retained picture host across covers.
   Supply thumbnails and playback pictures as bounded SDK views, without page builders.
4. Construct `makeOperatorMedia` from a `MonitorMediaLibraryModel` with the integration's
   explicit catalog/cache codec/address/eligibility facts. Return `MonitorOperatorMediaModel`
   with a factory for a fresh `MonitorMediaPlaybackModel` and an actual SDK player port.
   The scene binds the optional public `CameraMediaService` and owns gallery/item retirement.
5. Fill the optional `atlas-registration.example.json` with the authorized repository URL,
   reviewed app commit and the registered shared ref. Register the real app in `atlas/repos/<id>/meta.json`, add its `SUMMARY.md` and release
   compatibility record, then run `atlas check --sources`, the generated checks and the
   integration's consumer checks. Use a real authorized repository URL and published shared
   pin. The generator does not create a Git repository, registry row, remote or release.

A brand name is presentation content. Hardware differences arrive through capabilities,
descriptors, optional services and typed extension slots. Move common behavior into OpenCine
and verify the consumer pin rather than adding copied monitor, controls, settings or media code.

## SIMULATED isolation

The sample FakeIntegration implements the public Core/Media contracts in the app's developer
source sets. It has no shipping TestSupport or harness dependency, no process-wide session and
no manufacturer assumptions. Packaged synthetic footage is excluded from shipping targets.
Android disables `simulatedRelease`; the manual release workflow builds only shipping variants.
Saved cameras, assist/operator/display preferences, favorites and cached media live under this
app's own bundle/package sandbox and explicit `__NAME__` storage keys. UI reset flags only
clear the owned SIMULATED test installation. Removing the developer variant leaves production
integration configuration and complete shared screen composition intact.
